---
// Merges the former Finance and Operations pages into one advisory
// offering. This is a compression, not a concatenation: fundraising support
// and the fractional-CFO comparison table (which sold a standalone
// finance-leadership product) are dropped, and the maturity model plus
// decision-quality framing are kept and pulled up a level, because both are
// fundamentally about sequencing a decision against what can be measured.
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navigation from '@/components/Navigation.astro';
import Footer from '@/components/Footer.astro';
import SectionHero from '@/components/SectionHero.astro';
import StickySubnav from '@/components/StickySubnav.astro';
import KeyFactsBlock from '@/components/KeyFactsBlock.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '@/components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import EvidenceStandard from '@/components/EvidenceStandard.astro';
import FAQBlock from '@/components/FAQBlock.astro';
import PageByline from '@/components/PageByline.astro';
import ScrollReveal from '@/components/ScrollReveal.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { buildFaqPageSchema, buildServiceSchema, SITE_URL } from '@/lib/schema';

const allPosts = await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => !data.draft);
// No slice — see the identical comment in src/pages/seo.astro. Only 2 posts
// are in this category today so the cap wasn't yet starving anything here,
// but removing it now means it can't quietly do so once more are added.
const relatedPosts = allPosts
  .filter((p) => p.data.category === 'ai-strategy')
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'maturity', label: 'Adoption Maturity' },
  { id: 'modeling', label: 'Modeling & Decisions' },
  { id: 'tooling', label: 'Tool Selection' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What does an AI strategy engagement actually produce?',
    answer:
      'A sequenced plan for which processes change first, a financial model showing what the change is worth, and, if the engagement includes it, the tool selection and rollout that follow from both.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is this the same as hiring a fractional CFO or an ops consultant?',
    answer:
      'It overlaps with both but isn\'t either. We build the model and sequence the adoption; we don\'t take on broad financial leadership or run day-to-day operations. Most clients keep or hire their own finance and ops leads, and we work alongside them.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does "AI-assisted" mean in the modeling work?',
    answer:
      'AI tooling shortens the cycle time between a question and a modeled answer, scenario iteration, variance drafting, that kind of thing. It doesn\'t replace the underlying judgment. The model and its assumptions are still built and owned by a person, and a model built on messy data will produce a fast, confident, wrong answer just as easily as a slow one.',
  },
];
const faqSchema = buildFaqPageSchema(faqItems);

const serviceSchema = buildServiceSchema({
  id: `${SITE_URL}/ai-strategy#service`,
  name: 'AI Strategy Consulting',
  serviceType: 'Management Consulting',
  description:
    'AI adoption sequencing and financial modeling for growth-stage companies deciding where AI tooling actually pays for itself.',
});
---

<Layout
  title="AI Strategy Consulting | NCR Digital"
  description="AI adoption strategy and financial modeling: sequencing which processes change, and sizing what the change is worth, before anyone buys a tool."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} slot="head" />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} slot="head" />

  <Navigation />
  <main id="main-content">
    <SectionHero
      eyebrow="Capability"
      title="Know what AI is worth before you adopt it"
      subtitle="Advisory work on sequencing AI adoption and modeling what it's actually worth, so tooling decisions follow a business case instead of a trend."
      size="lg"
    />

    <StickySubnav sections={sections} />

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
      {/* This page is genuinely new — it replaces the deleted
          /capabilities/finance and /capabilities/operations rather than
          succeeding either one, so it publishes today rather than
          inheriting either page's old date. */}
      <PageByline author="Niekos Robbins" publishedDate={new Date('2026-08-11')} />
    </div>

    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-16 space-y-16">
      <section id="overview">
        <p class="text-lg text-ncr-text leading-relaxed">
          Most AI adoption fails for one of two reasons: a company changes too much at once and
          nothing sticks, or nobody built the model to know whether the change was worth the cost in
          the first place. This is advisory work on both: sequencing which processes change, and
          sizing what the change is worth before anyone commits budget to it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ncr-muted leading-relaxed mt-6">
          The two problems are related. A company can find a process worth changing and still not
          know whether it can afford to change it, or in what order, without a financial model behind
          the decision. We build that model and the adoption sequence together, rather than treating
          them as two separate engagements.
        </p>
      </section>

      <ScrollReveal>
      <section id="maturity">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-display-md text-ncr-text mb-4">
          How do you sequence AI adoption?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-ncr-text leading-relaxed">
          Across four stages, moving from individual tool use to an embedded process. Skipping stages
          is the most common cause of failed rollouts: a company licenses five tools for five teams
          at once, nothing sticks, and the conclusion is "AI doesn't work for us" when the actual
          problem was sequencing.
        </p>
        <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-ncr-muted">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-ncr-accent flex-shrink-0">1.</span>
            <span><strong class="text-ncr-text">Individual augmentation.</strong> Specific people use AI tools to do their existing job faster, no process change required.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-ncr-accent flex-shrink-0">2.</span>
            <span><strong class="text-ncr-text">Team workflow redesign.</strong> One team's process is rebuilt around the tooling, with clear ownership and a defined human-review handoff.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-ncr-accent flex-shrink-0">3.</span>
            <span><strong class="text-ncr-text">Cross-functional integration.</strong> The redesigned workflow connects to adjacent teams' systems, once stage 2 is proven in at least one team.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-ncr-accent flex-shrink-0">4.</span>
            <span><strong class="text-ncr-text">Embedded operating model.</strong> The AI-assisted process is the default way the work happens, with governance built in rather than bolted on.</span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
      </ScrollReveal>

      <ScrollReveal>
      <section id="modeling">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-display-md text-ncr-text mb-4">
          How do you decide what a stage is worth?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-ncr-text leading-relaxed">
          Before moving from one stage to the next, we build the model that answers whether it's
          worth it: scenario and sensitivity analysis on what changes if the workflow scales, and a
          monthly variance check tying the result back to what was forecast. AI tooling shortens how
          long that modeling takes, not the judgment behind it. A model built on messy, uncategorized
          data will produce a fast, confident, wrong answer just as easily as a slow one, so the data
          structure gets fixed before any AI-assisted iteration runs on top of it.
        </p>
      </section>
      </ScrollReveal>

      <ScrollReveal>
      <section id="tooling">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-display-md text-ncr-text mb-4">
          How do you choose which tools to adopt?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-ncr-text leading-relaxed">
          From the workflow bottleneck, not the vendor landscape. We identify the specific process
          that's slow or error-prone first, then evaluate tools against that defined need. That
          avoids the common failure of licensing a broad platform that no team ends up using because
          it was never selected to solve a problem anyone actually had. Enablement, training, usage
          guidelines, and a feedback loop that catches where a workflow is quietly being avoided, is
          what makes the redesign stick after the tool is chosen.
        </p>
      </section>
      </ScrollReveal>

      <EvidenceStandard
        items={[
          'No stage of the adoption sequence advances without a model showing what it\'s worth.',
          'Fix the underlying data structure before any AI-assisted scenario modeling runs on top of it.',
          'Tool selection starts from a measured bottleneck, never from a vendor pitch.',
        ]}
      />

      <KeyFactsBlock
        title="Key facts"
        facts={[
          'Adoption is sequenced across four stages: individual augmentation, team workflow redesign, cross-functional integration, embedded operating model.',
          'Most AI-adoption failures are sequencing failures, changing too much at once, not tooling failures.',
          'Every stage transition is backed by a financial model: scenario analysis, then a variance check against the forecast.',
          'Tool selection starts from a defined workflow bottleneck, never from the vendor landscape.',
        ]}
      />

      <ComparisonTable
        caption="Engagement fit by maturity stage"
        columns={['Best engagement model']}
        rows={[
          { label: 'Individual augmentation', cells: ['Project-based: a short tooling and training rollout.'] },
          { label: 'Team workflow redesign', cells: ['Project-based, often followed by a subscription for enablement.'] },
          { label: 'Cross-functional integration', cells: ['Subscription: ongoing coordination across teams.'] },
          { label: 'Embedded operating model', cells: ['Subscription: ongoing governance and iteration.'] },
        ]}
      />

      {relatedPosts.length > 0 && (
        <section aria-labelledby="related-heading">
          <h2 id="related-heading" class="font-display text-xl text-ncr-text mb-4">Related insights</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            {relatedPosts.map((post) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/insights/${post.slug}/`} class="text-ncr-accent underline decoration-ncr-accent/30 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-ncr-accent font-medium">
                  {post.data.title}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      )}

      <div id="faq">
        <FAQBlock items={faqItems} />
      </div>

      <div>
        <a
          href="/contact"
          class="inline-flex px-6 py-3 rounded-pill bg-ncr-accent text-white text-sm font-medium hover:bg-ncr-accentHover transition-all duration-200"
        >
          Talk to us about AI strategy
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
